    "text": "A refrigerator works by moving heat, not by making “cold.” A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. First, it absorbs heat from inside the fridge and turns into a gas. Then a compressor squeezes that gas, making it hot. That heat escapes through coils on the back or bottom of the fridge. Next, the refrigerant cools down and turns back into a liquid, ready to soak up more heat. This cycle repeats over and over. Insulation in the walls helps trap the cold air inside, while the motor keeps the cycle going. So your food stays cold because the fridge keeps carrying its heat away!"
  }
